    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Observer</h2>
        <p>
            Observer can be a human being or a measuring instrument. We <em>attach</em> a reference frame to the observer. Generally, the observer is assumed to be placed at origin O of the reference frame.
        </p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Position of a particle</h2>
        <p>Position of a particle can only be specified from within a frame of reference. It is specified by giving the point in the co-ordinate frame occupied by the particle at any time {`\\(t\\)`}. That is, if we say that the particle is at {`\\((x, y)\\)`} at time {`\\(t\\)`}, where {`\\(x\\)`} and {`\\(y\\)`} are the co-ordinates. Both {`\\(x\\)`} and {`\\(y\\)`} have dimensions of length. The particle is said to be at <em>rest</em> if {`\\((x, y)\\)`} does not change with time, otherwise, the particle is said to be in <em>motion</em>. </p>
        <p>Position can be specified using a vector whose tail is at the origin and head is at the location of the particle (as shown in the above figures). In general, we use symbol {`\\(\\vec{r}\\)`} to represent the position vector. {`\\[\\vec{r} = x\\hat{i} + y\\hat{j}\\]`} </p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Motion is relative</h2>
        <p>
            If position of an object is changing, we say that the object is moving <em>with respect to</em> the observer. There can be multiple observers. One observer can be moving with respect to the other.
        </p>
